-- YMD: the ISO 8601-ordered, BOUNDS-CHECKED date constructor.
--
-- `YMD y m d` agrees with `Date d m y` on every valid date. On an
-- out-of-range component it stops evaluation, where `Date` rolls forward.

IMPORT daydate


§ `YMD agrees with Date`

-- expected: DATE OF 28, 7, 2026
#EVAL YMD 2026 7 28

-- expected: TRUE
#EVAL YMD 2026 7 28 EQUALS Date 28 7 2026

-- expected: TRUE, the AKA is the same constructor
#EVAL `Year month day` 2026 7 28 EQUALS YMD 2026 7 28

-- expected: 740189, days since 1st January 0000
#EVAL DATE_SERIAL (YMD 2026 7 28)


§ `The boundary days are valid`

-- expected: DATE OF 31, 12, 2026
#EVAL YMD 2026 12 31

-- expected: DATE OF 1, 1, 2026
#EVAL YMD 2026 1 1

-- expected: DATE OF 29, 2, 2024, a real leap day
#EVAL YMD 2024 2 29

-- expected: DATE OF 29, 2, 2000, divisible by 400
#EVAL YMD 2000 2 29

-- expected: DATE OF 31, 1, 2026
#EVAL YMD 2026 1 31


§ `Date rolls where YMD stops`

-- `Date` is the lenient constructor: an out-of-range component rolls forward.

-- expected: DATE OF 1, 3, 2023, no 29 February in 2023
#EVAL Date 29 2 2023

-- expected: DATE OF 7, 4, 2028, month 28 overflows 2026 by 16 months
#EVAL Date 7 28 2026

-- expected: FALSE, the transposed form is a different date
#EVAL Date 7 28 2026 EQUALS YMD 2026 7 28

-- YMD stops on the same inputs.
-- expected: an assumed-term error naming `YMD refusal`
#EVAL YMD 2023 2 29
